<div data-testid="conversation-turn-1" data-message-author-role="user">
    <div class="prose"><p>Why does Rust not need a garbage collector?</p></div>
</div>
<div data-testid="conversation-turn-2" data-message-author-role="assistant">
    <div class="markdown prose">
        <p>Rust achieves memory safety without a garbage collector through its compile-time <b>ownership system</b> and borrow checker.</p>
        <h2>Core Rules</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Each value in Rust has an owner.</li>
            <li>There can only be one owner at a time.</li>
            <li>When the owner goes out of scope, the value is dropped.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>
<div data-testid="conversation-turn-3" data-message-author-role="user">
    <div class="prose"><p>What happens when data is borrowed?</p></div>
</div>
<div data-testid="conversation-turn-4" data-message-author-role="assistant">
    <div class="markdown prose">
        <p>Borrowing lets you reference data without taking ownership. You can have either:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Any number of immutable references (<code>&amp;T</code>).</li>
            <li>Exactly one mutable reference (<code>&amp;mut T</code>) at a time.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>
